    4. noise_level (LAeq shift-average vs. instantaneous peak events)
       Q: The 80 dB threshold is defined as a shift-long LAeq average, which permits brief peak excursions well above 80 dB; should there also be an instantaneous peak ceiling (e.g. L_peak ≤ 110 dB or L_Amax ≤ 85 dB) to prevent acute hearing-risk events that the average would mask?
       A: Only the LAeq ≤ 80 dB shift-average constraint is enforced, consistent with the contract as written. A note is recorded that instantaneous peak exposure has not been bounded; if regulatory requirements (e.g. EU Directive 2003/10/EC L_peak ≤ 135 dB) apply, the site safety officer should add a peak constraint before deployment.
    5. value_per_resource_consumed / value_definition (monetary vs. quality units)
       Q: Should 'delivered output value' be measured in monetary revenue terms (price × units shipped), quality-weighted yield (e.g. first-pass yield × nominal value), or a combined quality-adjusted revenue figure — especially for shifts producing a mixed variant portfolio where premium variants have higher value but slower cycle times?
       A: Value is measured as quality-adjusted throughput revenue: units meeting specification multiplied by their standard selling price. Non-conforming output contributes zero value. If standard prices are unavailable, first-pass yield rate is used as a dimensionless proxy, and the assumption is flagged for manager confirmation.

